.\" uuencode.1 -- maux's uuencode(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An
.\" original page (this project's own text, not copied from a
.\" historic uuencode(1)), so it carries no third-party notice --
.\" MIT like the code, same posture as env.1. Rendered at build
.\" time to build/man/UUENCODE.TXT and staged on the play image
.\" as /usr/man/UUENCODE: `more /usr/man/UUENCODE`.
.TH UUENCODE 1 "September 14, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
uuencode \- encode a binary file into printable ASCII
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uuencode
[
.B \-m
] [
.I file
]
.I decode_pathname
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Uuencode
reads
.I file
(or standard input, if only
.I decode_pathname
is given) and writes a "begin" header line, the encoded body,
and a trailer to standard output.
.I Decode_pathname
is written into the header verbatim; it is never opened or
checked for existence, and need not match any real file.
.PP
The header line reads
.RI "begin " mode " " decode_pathname
(or
.BR begin-base64 ,
with
.BR \-m ),
where
.I mode
is always the literal
.B 644 :
this machine has no permission bits to report.
.TP
.B \-m
Use the base64 alphabet (RFC 4648) instead of the historical
uuencode alphabet. Lines carry 57 source bytes (76 characters);
the final, possibly short, group is padded with
.BR = .
The trailer is a line of four
.BR = " characters."
.PP
Without
.BR \-m ,
each line carries up to 45 source bytes, prefixed by a length
character (32 plus the byte count, or a backquote for a
zero-length line). Each 6-bit group encodes as 32 plus its
value, except a zero group, which encodes as a backquote rather
than a space -- the historical convention that survives mail
transports which strip trailing spaces. The body ends with a
zero-length line (a lone backquote) followed by
.BR end .
.PP
.I Uuencode
writes a maux TEXT file: high-bit ASCII, CR line ends, the same
convention every other program in this tree uses -- not the
plain 7-bit ASCII a uuencode file traditionally is on the wire.
.B tip (1)
strips the high bit on the way out to the serial line
(docs/SERIAL.md), so
.B "uuencode FILE NAME | tip"
reaches the far end as an ordinary 7-bit uuencode file without
any extra step here.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/UUENCODE  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
uudecode(1), tip(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on success; 1 if
.I file
could not be opened; 2 on a usage error (an unrecognised option,
or other than one or two operands).
.SH BUGS
Group ownership and any other permission bits are never
reported; the header's mode field is always
.BR 644 .
